.Dt MK 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mk
.Nd scope-based build automation tool
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl hkpsSv
.Op Fl C Ar dir
.Op Fl f Ar makefile
.Op Fl o Ar objdir
.Op Fl V Ar var
.Op Ar target ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a build automation tool in the spirit of
.Xr make 1 ,
designed for larger projects where recursive make or a single giant makefile
become unmanageable.
.Pp
Unlike traditional
.Xr make 1 ,
.Nm
treats targets and prerequisites as
paths rather than plain strings, and arranges every directory of the source
tree into a
.Em scope .
Scopes form a tree rooted at the top-level directory; subdirectories are
parsed lazily, only when a target inside them is required.
This model removes the need for recursive
.Nm
invocations while still keeping per-directory state.
.Pp
The following options are available:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fl C Ar dir
Change the working directory to
.Ar dir
before doing anything else.
.It Fl f Ar makefile
Read
.Ar makefile
instead of the default makefile.
Contrary to other implementations, only the last occurrence of this option
is honored.
.It Fl h
Print a help page listing the user-visible macros and targets of the current
directory (those carrying documentation comments), then exit.
When combined with
.Fl v ,
the help page is produced recursively for every subdirectory.
.It Fl k
Continue processing after errors are encountered, but only on targets that
do not depend on the target whose recipe failed.
This is the opposite of
.Fl S .
.It Fl o Ar objdir
Place generated artifacts into
.Ar objdir
and search there for existing artifacts.
Useful for out-of-tree builds.
The path is exposed to makefiles through
.Ev .OBJDIR .
.It Fl p
Dump the parsed state of the current directory's makefile (macros, targets,
rules, inference rules, and subdirectories) and exit.
When combined with
.Fl v ,
the dump is produced recursively for every subdirectory.
.It Fl s
Do not echo commands before executing them.
Equivalent to prefixing every command with
.Sq Ic @ .
This option overrides
.Fl v .
.It Fl S
Stop processing as soon as a command returns a non-zero status.
This is the default behavior and is the opposite of
.Fl k .
.It Fl V Ar var
Expand
.Ar var
as if it were written
.Li ${ Ns Ar var Ns Li }
and print the result to standard output, then exit.
If
.Ar var
already contains a
.Ql $
character it is expanded verbatim instead, allowing arbitrary expressions
such as
.Li "${SOURCE:M*.c}" .
.It Fl v
Increase verbosity.
May be specified multiple times.
At higher levels,
.Nm
also descends into subdirectories for
.Fl h
and
.Fl p ,
and prints extra status while parsing.
.El
.Sh DEPENDENCY LINES
A dependency line consists of one or more targets followed by a colon and
zero or more prerequisites:
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
.Ar target ... : Ns Op Ar prerequisite ...
.Ed
.Pp
This declares that each
.Ar target
depends on every
.Ar prerequisite
and is to be (re)built from them.
.Pp
Targets and prerequisites are interpreted as paths.
They must be relative and may not escape the build tree.
.Sh SHELL COMMANDS
A dependency line may be followed by a series of indented shell commands
\(em the target's recipe \(em used to build the target.
While several dependency lines may name the same target, only one of them
may carry a recipe.
.Pp
If a command line begins with one of the characters
.Sq Ic @
or
.Sq Ic \- ,
the command is treated specially:
.Bl -tag -width '@'
.It Sq Ic @
Causes the command not to be echoed before it is executed.
.It Sq Ic \-
Causes any non-zero exit status of the command to be ignored.
.El
.Pp
Each command line is executed by
.Pa /bin/sh
with
.Ic set -e
in effect, unless
.Sq Ic \-
was given (or the platform shell is known to be broken, as on Minix-vmd).
A separate shell is spawned for each command line.
.Sh INFERENCE RULES
An inference rule is a recipe that knows how to produce a file with one
suffix from a file with another suffix.
They are written as dependency lines whose target has the form
.Li . Ns Ar from Ns Li . Ns Ar to ,
where each of
.Ar from
and
.Ar to
is a dot-prefixed suffix of at most three characters (for example
.Li \&.c.o ,
.Li \&.y.c ,
.Li \&.l.c ) :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&.c.o:
	${CC} ${CFLAGS} -c $< -o $@
.Ed
.Pp
When
.Nm
needs a target
.Pa foo. Ns Ar to
that has no explicit recipe, it searches the current scope and its
ancestors for an inference rule whose
.Ar to
matches.
If a source file
.Pa foo. Ns Ar from
exists, the inference rule is instantiated:
a synthetic prerequisite on
.Pa foo. Ns Ar from
is added, and the rule's recipe is attached.
Inference rules may also list their own prerequisites after the colon.
Unlike
.Xr make 1 ,
no
.Ic .SUFFIXES:
declaration is required and inference chains may be arbitrarily deep.
.Pp
Inside an inference rule's recipe, the special variables
.Ev .TARGET
.Pq Sq Ic $@
and
.Ev .IMPSRC
.Pq Sq Ic $<
refer to the produced target and the inferred source respectively.
.Sh VARIABLE ASSIGNMENTS
Variables in
.Nm
\(em also called
.Em macros
\(em are assigned with:
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
.Ar name No <op> Ar value
.Ed
.Pp
Macro names consist of the characters
.Sq [a-zA-Z0-9_.]
and may not be empty.
By convention, names use all upper-case letters.
The following operators are recognised:
.Bl -tag -width "Ic ??="  -compact
.It Ic \&=
Assign the value lazily; it is expanded each time the macro is referenced.
Any previous value is overridden.
.It Ic \&:=
Expand the right-hand side immediately, then assign the result.
.It Ic \&::=
Currently equivalent to
.Ic := .
.It Ic \&+=
Append the value to the current value of the macro.
A single space is inserted between the previous contents and the appended
value.
.It Ic \&?=
Assign the value only if the macro is not already defined in any reachable
scope.
.It Ic \&??=
Assign the value if the macro is neither already defined nor present in the
environment.
This is the typical way to let
.Ev CC ,
.Ev CFLAGS ,
etc. be overridden from the shell.
.It Ic \&!=
Expand the right-hand side, pass it to the shell, and assign its standard
output to the macro.
.El
.Pp
Whitespace surrounding
.Ar value
is stripped before assignment.
.Pp
Macros are expanded by surrounding the name with curly braces
.Pq Ql {}
preceded by a dollar sign
.Pq Ql $ :
.Li ${NAME} .
If the name is a single character, the braces may be omitted, as in
.Li $@ .
A literal dollar sign is written
.Li $$ .
.Pp
Macros are made visible to foreign subdirectories (those declared with
.Ic .FOREIGN: )
through the
.Ic .EXPORTS:
directive.
.Ss SPECIAL VARIABLES
The following variables have a built-in meaning:
.Bl -tag -width "Ev .MAKEFILES" -compact
.It Ev $$\&
A literal dollar sign.
.It Ev \&.TOPDIR
Relative path from the current directory to the top-level directory.
Shortcut:
.Sq Ic $. .
.It Ev \&.TARGET
The name of the target currently being built.
Shortcut:
.Sq Ic $@ .
.It Ev \&.IMPSRC
The inferred source prerequisite of the current target, when an inference
rule applies.
Shortcut:
.Sq Ic $< .
.It Ev \&.ALLSRC
All prerequisites of the current target, space-separated.
Shortcut:
.Sq Ic $^ .
.It Ev \&.IMPSRC:T
The
.Xr basename 1
portion of
.Ev .IMPSRC .
Shortcut:
.Sq Ic $* .
.It Ev \&.OBJDIR
The object directory selected with
.Fl o ,
or the current directory.
.It Ev \&.SUBDIRS
The list of subdirectories declared with
.Ic .SUBDIRS: .
.It Ev \&.EXPORTS
The list of macros declared with
.Ic .EXPORTS: ,
rendered as
.Li NAME='value'
pairs suitable for prefixing a foreign build command.
.It Ev \&.MAKEFILES
The list of makefiles seen on the path from the root to the current
directory.
.It Ev SHELL
The shell used to execute recipes
.Pq default Pa /bin/sh .
.It Ev MAKE
The name under which
.Nm
was invoked.
Also available as
.Ev .MAKE .
.It Ev MAKEFLAGS
A snapshot of the command-line options passed to
.Nm .
Also available as
.Ev .MAKEFLAGS .
.El
.Ss MODIFIERS
Modifiers transform the expansion of a macro.
They are chained after the name with
.Ql : :
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
.Li ${ Ns Ar NAME Ns Li : Ns Ar modifier ...
.Ed
.Pp
The following modifiers are available:
.Bl -tag -width "Ic :Mpattern" -compact
.It Ic :U
Convert every character to upper case.
.It Ic :L
Convert every character to lower case.
.It Ic :F
For each word, search for a matching file in either the source directory or
.Ev .OBJDIR .
Used to locate build artifacts when building out-of-tree.
.It Ic :E
Replace each word by its suffix (file extension).
.It Ic :R
Replace each word by everything but its suffix.
.It Ic :H
Replace each word by its
.Xr dirname 1 .
.It Ic :T
Replace each word by its
.Xr basename 1 .
.It Ic :M Ar pattern
Retain only words matching
.Ar pattern .
.It Ic :N Ar pattern
The opposite of
.Ic :M .
.It Ic :J Ar separator
Join all words into a single string, separated by
.Ar separator .
.It Ic \&: Ns Ar old Ns = Ns Ar new
Replace every occurrence of
.Ar old
with
.Ar new .
This must be the last modifier in an expansion.
.El
.Pp
A worked example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
SOURCE = main.c lex.l parse.y gen.S

# Only retain words matching the glob *.c
CFILES = ${SOURCE:M*.c}

# Replace the .c extension with .o
COBJS = ${CFILES:.c=.o}

clean:
	# Use :F to find the objects in ${.OBJDIR}
	rm -f ${COBJS:F}

print:
	# Multiple modifiers may be combined.
	@echo ${SOURCE:T:U}
.Ed
.Sh INCLUDE STATEMENTS
The
.Ic include
statement reads another file at that point:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# Require templates.mk
include templates.mk

# Include config.mk only if it exists
-include config.mk
# "sinclude" is a synonym for "-include"
sinclude local.mk
.Ed
.Pp
Included paths that are not absolute are resolved relative to the current
directory's scope.
.Sh CONDITIONALS
Sections of a makefile may be conditionally parsed using the directives
.Ic .if ,
.Ic .elif ,
.Ic .else ,
and
.Ic .endif ,
which may be nested up to 16 levels deep.
The argument to
.Ic .if
and
.Ic .elif
is a boolean expression:
.Bd -ragged -offset indent
.Li .if Ar expr
.Ed
.Pp
Expressions are composed of:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
Quoted string literals,
.Li "text" .
.It
Macro references,
.Li ${NAME} .
.It
The predicates
.Li defined(NAME)
(true if the macro
.Ar NAME
exists in a reachable scope) and
.Li target(NAME)
(true if a target named
.Ar NAME
is defined in the current scope).
.It
A backtick command substitution,
.Li \&`command arg1 arg2\&` ,
which runs the command via the shell and expands to its standard output
(trailing newlines stripped).
The command is macro-expanded before execution.
A non-zero exit status is a fatal error.
.It
The comparison operators
.Cm == , != , < , <= , > , >= .
If both operands look like integers, comparison is numeric; otherwise it is
lexicographic.
.It
The logical operators
.Cm && ,
.Cm || ,
and unary
.Cm ! .
.El
.Pp
Example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&.if "${OS}" == "Linux"
CFLAGS += -DLINUX
\&.elif defined(DEBUG)
CFLAGS += -g
\&.else
CFLAGS += -O2
\&.endif
\&.if `uname -s` == "OpenBSD"
LDFLAGS += -static
\&.endif
.Ed
.Sh SUBDIRECTORIES
Subdirectories are declared with the
.Ic .SUBDIRS:
directive:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&.SUBDIRS: foo bar
.Ed
.Pp
Macro references in the directory list are expanded before the list is
processed, so
.Ql .SUBDIRS: ${SUBDIRS}
is valid.
Each named directory becomes a child scope of the current scope and is
parsed lazily: its makefile is read only when a target inside that subtree
is required.
The list may be referenced as
.Ev .SUBDIRS ,
for example to recurse a target into every subdirectory:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
clean: ${.SUBDIRS:=/clean}
.Ed
.Pp
A prerequisite may name a path in any subdirectory, in which case
.Nm
descends into the corresponding scope automatically:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
foobar: foo/libfoo.a bar/libbar.so
	ar rcs $@ $^
.Ed
.Pp
Subdirectories referenced as prerequisites do not need to be listed in
.Ic .SUBDIRS: .
If the directory exists on the filesystem but is not declared,
.Nm
resolves the file against the real filesystem directly: it checks
that the file exists and uses its modification time, but does not
read the subdirectory's makefile and does not apply any build rules.
To allow files in a subdirectory to be built rather than only read,
the subdirectory must be declared with
.Ic .SUBDIRS: .
.Ss FOREIGN SUBDIRECTORIES
A subdirectory driven by a different build system (GNU make, Autotools,
BSD make, etc.) is declared with
.Ic .FOREIGN: :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&.FOREIGN: hello
.Ed
.Pp
Macro references in the directory list are expanded before the list is
processed.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&.FOREIGN: hello
HELLODIR = hello
EMAKE ?= make
\&.EXPORTS: CC CFLAGS PREFIX DESTDIR
.Ed
.Pp
Because such subdirectories do not contain a
.Nm
makefile, interaction happens entirely through ordinary targets whose
recipes shell out to the foreign build system.
Macros listed in
.Ic .EXPORTS:
are rendered through
.Ev .EXPORTS
as
.Li NAME='value'
assignments suitable for passing on the foreign tool's command line:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
hello!: .hello-configure
	${EMAKE} -C ${HELLODIR:F} ${.EXPORTS}

clean: hello/clean
	rm -f xhello
.Ed
.Pp
Targets suffixed with
.Sq ?
or
.Sq !
are dispatched to the foreign scope's
.Em test
and
.Em exec
hooks respectively (see the
.Sx EXAMPLES
section for a complete integration).
.Pp
A dependency line whose targets are bare foreign subdirectory names
(without
.Sq ?
or
.Sq ! )
declares build ordering without supplying a recipe.
The named prerequisites are built before either hook of the foreign
subdirectory runs.
This is useful when one foreign subdirectory depends on another:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&.FOREIGN: liba libb

libb: liba

liba!:
	${MAKE} -C liba ${<}

libb!:
	${MAKE} -C libb ${<}
.Ed
.Sh OTHER DIRECTIVES
.Bl -tag -width ".Ic .endtemplate" -compact
.It Ic include
See
.Sx INCLUDE STATEMENTS .
.It Ic .template Ar name No ... Ic .endt
Define a named block of text (terminated by
.Ic .endt
or
.Ic .endtemplate )
that can be inlined elsewhere via
.Ic .expand .
Useful for factoring out boilerplate that recurs across makefiles.
.It Ic .expand Ar name
Inline the named template at this point, parsing its body as makefile input.
.El
.Sh COMMENTS
A comment begins with a single hash
.Pq Ql \&#
character and runs to the end of the line.
Inside a recipe, the hash is passed to the shell unchanged.
.Ss DOCUMENTATION COMMENTS
A documentation comment is a line beginning with two hashes
.Pq Ql \&## .
The text that follows is attached as help text to the next rule or macro
definition and is shown by
.Fl h .
Macro references inside a doc-comment are expanded, so the comment always
reflects the current value of the variables it mentions.
.Pp
Example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
## Name of the project
NAME = hello

## Build ${NAME}
${NAME}: ${NAME}.c
	${CC} -o $@ $<
.Ed
.Sh SPECIAL TARGETS
.Bl -tag -width ".Ic .SUBDIRS:" -compact
.It Ic .DEFAULT: Ar target
Set the default goal that is built when
.Nm
is invoked without any
.Ar target
arguments.
.It Ic .SUBDIRS: Ar dir ...
Declare each
.Ar dir
as a child scope of the current directory.
Macro references in
.Ar dir
are expanded.
.It Ic .FOREIGN: Ar dir ...
Declare each
.Ar dir
as a foreign subdirectory driven by an external build system.
Macro references in
.Ar dir
are expanded.
.It Ic .EXPORTS: Ar name ...
Mark each macro
.Ar name
as exported to foreign subdirectories.
The macros must already be defined when this directive is encountered.
.It Ic .POSIX:
Accepted for compatibility; emits a warning reminding the user that
.Nm
is not a POSIX make.
.It Ic .SUFFIXES:
Accepted for compatibility; emits a warning that
.Nm
does not need it.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Nm
does not import the entire environment into the macro namespace.
Instead, individual macros can opt in to environment lookup via the
.Ic ??=
operator.
.Pp
The
.Ev MAKE
and
.Ev MAKEFLAGS
variables are populated by
.Nm
itself and propagated to recursive invocations.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Mkfile -compact
.It Pa Mkfile
Default makefile, unless overridden by
.Fl f
or the
.Fl -with-makefile
option of
.Pa configure .
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
The following is a sketch of a project that integrates an Autotools-driven
dependency
.Pa hello
into a
.Nm
build:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&.FOREIGN: hello
HELLODIR = hello
EMAKE ?= make
CC ?= cc
CFLAGS ?=
PREFIX ?= /usr/local
DESTDIR ?=
\&.EXPORTS: CC CFLAGS DESTDIR

all: hello/hello xhello

clean: hello/clean
	rm -f xhello

distclean: clean
	rm -rf ${HELLODIR:F}
	rm -f ${.OBJDIR}/{hello.tgz,.hello-configure,.hello-extract}

install: hello/install xhello
	mkdir -p ${DESTDIR}${PREFIX}/bin
	cp -f ${.OBJDIR}/xhello ${DESTDIR}${PREFIX}/bin/

hello.tgz:
	ftp -o $@ https://got.stuerz.xyz/download/hello-2.0.tgz

\&.hello-extract: hello.tgz
	tar -xzf $< -C ${.OBJDIR}
	touch $@

\&.hello-configure: .hello-extract
	(cd ${HELLODIR:F} && ./configure --prefix=${PREFIX})
	touch $@

# Is $< up to date in hello?
hello?:
	test -e ${HELLODIR:F}/Makefile
	${EMAKE} -q -C ${HELLODIR:F} ${.EXPORTS} "$$(echo "$<" | sed 's#\\./##')"

# Build $< in hello
hello!: .hello-configure
	${EMAKE} -C ${HELLODIR:F} ${.EXPORTS} "$$(echo "$<" | sed 's#\\./##')"

xhello: xhello.c hello/libhello.a
	${CC} -o $@ xhello.c -I${HELLODIR:F} -L${HELLODIR:F} -lhello ${CFLAGS}
.Ed
.Pp
Real-world projects built with
.Nm
include
.Lk https://got.stuerz.xyz/?action=summary&path=desktop.git desktop
and
.Lk https://got.stuerz.xyz/?action=summary&path=286bsd.git 286bsd .
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr make 1 ,
.Xr sh 1
.Sh STANDARDS
This implementation of
.Xr make 1
does not strictly follow any POSIX standard, but most simple makefiles work
unchanged.
.Sh HISTORY
Over the long history of make there have been many competing
implementations, including the original PWB/UNIX
.Xr make 1 ,
GNU Make, the various BSD makes, and Plan 9's
.Xr mk 1 .
.Nm
draws inspiration from those, but replaces string-based targets with a
scope-based path tree to avoid recursive make.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Benjamin Stürz Aq Mt benni@stuerz.xyz
.Sh CAVEATS
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
All paths used as targets or prerequisites must be relative.
.It
The handling of
.Ic ?=
and
.Ic ??=
differs from POSIX.
.It
The way environment and command-line variables are treated differs from
POSIX; in particular, the environment is not imported as macros
automatically.
.El
.Sh BUGS
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
There exists another project called
.Xr mk 1 .
.It
Defining certain forms of recursive dependencies can crash
.Nm .
.It
Specifying multiple targets at once on a single dependency line is broken
in some cases.
.It
This manual is still being expanded.
.El
